  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Automatic module-cache maintenance now serializes canonical lock publication and whole-object retirement with a recoverable directory guard, so every ownership transition is atomic and crash remnants are reclaimable. Fresh and stale legacy regular-file locks migrate conservatively. Work-limited passes may remove one oversized eligible generation, continue past a failed candidate within a bounded attempt allowance, and record a shorter observable retry deadline whenever work remains or removal fails. State publication is atomic, startup remains silent, and `sagejs cache status [--json]` exposes the last result and next deadline. Deterministic fixtures cover two-process contention, teardown contention, empty-lock and empty-guard crashes, legacy migration, oversized progress, per-entry errors, deferred retry scheduling, protected generations, unsafe markers, and status output.",
    "risks": [
      "Like the existing advisory lease protocol, reclaiming an owner whose token has not changed for longer than the stale timeout assumes that owner is dead rather than paused.",
      "Compiler fixtures currently have unrelated failures on the integration branch: algebra exceeded its fixture timeout and matrix sparse-random iteration raised object-is-not-iterable. The cache changes' build, TypeScript, and focused tests pass."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Rerun pnpm test:compiler and pnpm test:integration after the integration branch's algebra timeout and matrix sparse-random regression are resolved.",
      "Exercise the directory-lock tests on native Windows CI, where mkdir/rmdir are the intended portable synchronization primitives."
    ]
  }
